---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import S from '../components/S.astro';
import T from '../components/T.astro';
import L from '../components/L.astro';
import Cite from '../components/Cite.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import Deeper from '../components/Deeper.astro';
import Widget from '../components/Widget.astro';
import SpecTable from '../components/SpecTable.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Fat-Trees & Rails"
  description="Switch radix sets how many endpoints fit, oversubscription is what you sell to save money, and rail alignment is what makes a collective cheap."
  heading="Fat-Trees, Rails and Oversubscription"
  slug="/topology"
>
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="lede">
      A fabric's shape is not an aesthetic choice. Given a switch with a fixed number of ports,
      the shape determines how many endpoints you can reach, how many hops away they are, how much
      optics you buy, and whether an all-reduce runs at full speed or at a quarter of it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="fat-tree">Why a tree, and why a fat one</h2>

    <L at={1}>
      <p>
        Switches have a fixed number of sockets. A Q3400 has 144. If you need to connect more
        machines than that, you have to connect switches to each other — and now some of those 144
        sockets are used up talking to other switches instead of to machines.
      </p>
      <p>
        Arrange them in a tree and a problem appears: everything at the bottom eventually has to
        squeeze through the top. A <em>fat</em> tree fixes that by making the upper branches as
        thick as everything below them, so nothing narrows on the way up. That is more switches
        and far more cable than a thin tree — and it is the difference between a network that
        slows down when everyone talks at once and one that does not.
      </p>
    </L>

    <L min={2}>
      <p>
        The arithmetic is simple enough to do in your head. Take a switch of radix <em>k</em> and
        point half its ports down at endpoints and half up at the tier above. A two-tier fat tree
        then holds <em>k</em> × <em>k</em>/2 endpoints: at <em>k</em> = 144 that is
        144 × 72 = 10,368, which is why NVIDIA quotes
        <S id="fat-tree-2tier" /> for a two-level fat tree on
        Quantum-X800.<Cite ids="nv-ib-switching" />
      </p>
      <p>
        The property that matters is uniformity. Every endpoint is the same number of hops from
        every other, and every pair has a path that does not contend with every other pair. For a
        collective — whose completion time is set by its slowest participant — a uniform fabric
        is worth more than a fast but lumpy one.
      </p>
    </L>

    <L min={3}>
      <p>
        Radix is the lever with the most leverage, because tiers multiply cost non-linearly. Each
        additional tier adds two hops to every cross-fabric path, adds a full set of switches, and
        — since inter-tier links cross rows — turns copper into optics. Doubling radix from 72 to
        144 quadruples two-tier capacity, which is why switch vendors chase port count rather than
        per-port rate alone.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is also why NVIDIA's fabrics are not only fat trees. The reference architecture notes
        support for "fat-tree, Dragonfly, and multi-dimensional Torus" topologies.<Cite ids="nv-superpod-fabrics" />
        Fat trees dominate for AI because they are uniform and because collectives are the
        workload; the alternatives trade uniformity for cable count at very large scale.
      </p>
    </L>
  </div>

  <Widget
    title="Topology builder"
    hint="Set radix, tiers and oversubscription"
    caption="Standard fat-tree arithmetic, computed live rather than looked up: endpoints = k × (k/2)^(tiers−1) adjusted for the downward share, with switch and cable counts following. It is exact for the idealised topology and ignores the practicalities — spare ports for management, failure domains, and the fact that real builds round to whole racks."
  >
    <div data-topo>
      <svg class="diagram" viewBox="0 0 720 220" role="img"
        aria-label="A schematic fat tree whose tier count and width change with the selected radix and oversubscription, with the resulting endpoint, switch and cable counts.">
        <g data-tree></g>
      </svg>
      <dl class="readout" style="margin-top:0.75rem">
        <div><dt>Endpoints</dt><dd data-out="ep">10,368</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Leaf switches</dt><dd data-out="leaf">144</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Spine switches</dt><dd data-out="spine">72</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Inter-tier cables</dt><dd data-out="cables">10,368</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Worst-case bisection</dt><dd data-out="bisect">100%</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Hops, worst case</dt><dd data-out="hops">4</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p data-out="verdict" style="margin:0.9rem 0 0;font-size:0.875rem;color:var(--ink-2)"></p>
    </div>
    <div slot="controls" class="controls-row">
      <div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="Switch radix">
        <button type="button" data-radix="36" aria-pressed="false">Radix 36</button>
        <button type="button" data-radix="64" aria-pressed="false">Radix 64</button>
        <button type="button" data-radix="144" aria-pressed="true">Radix 144</button>
      </div>
      <div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="Tiers">
        <button type="button" data-tiers="2" aria-pressed="true">Two tiers</button>
        <button type="button" data-tiers="3" aria-pressed="false">Three tiers</button>
      </div>
      <div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="Oversubscription">
        <button type="button" data-over="1" aria-pressed="true">1:1</button>
        <button type="button" data-over="2" aria-pressed="false">2:1</button>
        <button type="button" data-over="4" aria-pressed="false">4:1</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Widget>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2 id="oversubscription">The thing you sell to save money</h2>

    <L min={2}>
      <p>
        <T id="oversubscription">Oversubscription</T> means fitting less upward capacity than
        downward — pointing more of each leaf's ports at endpoints and fewer at the tier above. A
        2:1 upper tier roughly halves the spine switches and the optics between tiers, which on a
        fabric of this size is a very large number.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is invisible to traffic that stays local, which is most enterprise traffic and almost
        none of this. An all-reduce is the pattern in which every endpoint transmits across the
        fabric simultaneously — precisely the case oversubscription bets against. Which is why AI
        fabrics are usually built non-blocking where general-purpose data-centre fabrics are not,
        and why the SuperPOD design is explicit about being "a fully non-blocking fat tree
        topology for each SU".<Cite ids="nv-superpod-fabrics" />
      </p>
    </L>

    <L min={3}>
      <p>
        <T id="bisection">Bisection bandwidth</T> is the honest measure and aggregate bandwidth is
        the flattering one. Aggregate counts every link in the fabric, including the ones a
        given traffic pattern will never use; bisection counts only what crosses the worst-case
        cut, which is exactly what an all-to-all has to push through.
      </p>
      <p>
        Note what <a href="/routing#adaptive">adaptive routing</a> can and cannot do about it. It
        chooses among paths that exist, so on an oversubscribed fabric it will distribute
        contention evenly across uplinks that are collectively too few. Even distribution of an
        inadequate resource is still inadequate. Oversubscription is a capacity decision, not a
        scheduling one, and no amount of cleverness at run time recovers it.
      </p>
    </L>

    <h2 id="rails">Rails: making the common case one hop</h2>

    <L at={1}>
      <p>
        Here is a trick that costs nothing and saves a great deal. Each compute tray has four
        network connections, one per GPU. Instead of wiring them arbitrarily, wire GPU number one
        in every rack to the same switch, GPU number two in every rack to a second switch, and so
        on.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now when the GPUs numbered one across the whole cluster want to talk to each other — which
        is exactly what they do during a collective — they are all already plugged into the same
        switch. The traffic never climbs the tree at all.
      </p>
    </L>

    <L min={2}>
      <p>
        That is <T id="rail-optimised">rail alignment</T>. A compute tray carries
        <S id="superpod-rails" /> — one per GPU — and racks are rail-aligned so that, in NVIDIA's
        words, "traffic per rail of each compute tray is always one hop away from other compute
        trays in the same Scalable Unit".<Cite ids="nv-superpod-fabrics" />
      </p>
      <p>
        The structure follows from it. Each Scalable Unit contains four spine-leaf groups, one per
        rail, and each group has <S id="slg-leaf" /> — one per compute rack — and
        <S id="slg-spine" />.<Cite ids="nv-superpod-fabrics" /> The rail count and the group count
        are the same number for the same reason.
      </p>
    </L>

    <L min={3}>
      <p>
        This is the scale-out restatement of the trick that makes the in-rack fabric single-layer.
        <a href="/nvlink#arithmetic">Chapter 5 of Part I</a> matches NVLink port <em>n</em> on
        every GPU to NVSwitch <em>n</em>, so any peer is one hop away. Rail alignment matches NIC
        <em>n</em> on every tray to leaf switch <em>n</em>, so any same-indexed peer is one hop
        away. Same reasoning, one tier out: collectives dominate, so build the topology the
        collective wants rather than a general-purpose one.
      </p>
      <p>
        It also pays for itself physically. A rail hop is tray-to-leaf, which the layout can
        usually keep inside <a href="/switches-optics#reach">the copper envelope</a>; a hop across
        tiers crosses rows and must go optical. Rail alignment converts the dominant traffic of a
        collective into the cheapest link in the building.
      </p>
    </L>

    <Callout kind="key">
      <p>
        Three levers, in descending order of how much they matter here: build it non-blocking,
        because collectives make everyone talk at once; align the rails, because that makes the
        dominant pattern a single hop; and pick the highest radix you can, because tiers cost two
        hops and a building's worth of optics each.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <h2 id="numbers">Topology figures</h2>

    <SpecTable
      caption="Fabric structure"
      ids={['fat-tree-2tier', 'superpod-rails', 'slg-leaf', 'slg-spine', 'superpod-bf3', 'leaf-switches', 'max-gpus']}
    />

    <Deeper title="One switch count this site will not state confidently">
      <p>
        The GB200 product page puts the maximum fabric at <S id="max-gpus" /> GPUs with 128 leaf
        switches. The DGX GB200 SuperPOD reference architecture, describing sixteen Scalable
        Units, implies a different arrangement — 64 leaf and 384 spine.<Cite ids={['nv-gb200', 'nv-superpod-fabrics']} />
      </p>
      <p>
        These are almost certainly two different reference designs rather than a contradiction: a
        product-page maximum and a specific validated architecture do not have to describe the
        same build. But "almost certainly" is not the standard this site holds itself to, so
        <S id="leaf-switches" /> is marked disputed and shows both. The practical instruction is
        the useful part: do not mix the two figures inside one calculation.
      </p>
    </Deeper>
  </div>

  <script>
    const root = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-topo]');
    if (root) {
      const fig = root.closest('figure')!;
      const NS = 'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg';
      const tree = root.querySelector('[data-tree]')!;
      const out = (k: string) => fig.querySelector<HTMLElement>(`[data-out="${k}"]`)!;
      const el = (n: string, a: Record<string, string | number>) => {
        const e = document.createElementNS(NS, n);
        for (const k in a) e.setAttribute(k, String(a[k]));
        return e;
      };

      let radix = 144, tiers = 2, over = 1;

      const model = () => {
        /*
         * Split each leaf's ports between endpoints and uplinks. At 1:1 the
         * split is even; an oversubscription of n:1 points n times as many
         * ports downward as upward.
         */
        const up = Math.floor(radix / (over + 1));
        const down = radix - up;

        if (tiers === 2) {
          const leaf = radix;               // limited by spine radix
          const spine = up;
          return {
            endpoints: leaf * down, leaf, spine,
            cables: leaf * up, hops: 4,
            bisection: 1 / over,
          };
        }
        // Three tiers: each pod is a two-tier fat tree, and pods attach to a core.
        const perPod = down * down;
        const pods = radix;
        return {
          endpoints: perPod * pods, leaf: pods * down, spine: pods * up,
          cables: pods * down * up * 2, hops: 6,
          bisection: 1 / over,
        };
      };

      const fmt = (n: number) => n.toLocaleString();

      const render = () => {
        const m = model();
        out('ep').textContent = fmt(m.endpoints);
        out('leaf').textContent = fmt(m.leaf);
        out('spine').textContent = fmt(m.spine);
        out('cables').textContent = fmt(m.cables);
        out('bisect').textContent = Math.round(m.bisection * 100) + '%';
        out('hops').textContent = String(m.hops);

        const parts: string[] = [];
        parts.push(`A radix-${radix} switch in ${tiers} tiers at ${over}:1 reaches ${fmt(m.endpoints)} endpoints.`);
        if (over === 1) {
          parts.push('Non-blocking: every endpoint can transmit across the fabric at once without contending, which is the only configuration an all-reduce is indifferent to.');
        } else {
          parts.push(`At ${over}:1 the upper tier carries ${Math.round(m.bisection * 100)}% of what the endpoints below it can offer. Local traffic will not notice; a collective will, because a collective is the case where everyone transmits at once.`);
        }
        if (tiers === 3) {
          parts.push('The third tier adds two hops to every cross-fabric path and a full set of switches — and those links cross rows, so they are optical rather than copper.');
        }
        out('verdict').textContent = parts.join(' ');

        // ── Draw ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        tree.textContent = '';
        const rows = tiers === 3
          ? [{ y: 20, n: 6, label: 'core', fill: 'var(--switch)' },
             { y: 76, n: 10, label: 'spine', fill: 'var(--switch)' },
             { y: 132, n: 14, label: 'leaf', fill: 'var(--cyan)' }]
          : [{ y: 40, n: 8, label: 'spine', fill: 'var(--switch)' },
             { y: 116, n: 14, label: 'leaf', fill: 'var(--cyan)' }];

        rows.forEach((row, ri) => {
          const w = 640 / row.n;
          for (let i = 0; i < row.n; i++) {
            tree.appendChild(el('rect', { x: 40 + i * w + 3, y: row.y, width: w - 6, height: 18,
              rx: 3, fill: row.fill, opacity: 0.85 }));
          }
          const t = el('text', { x: 32, y: row.y + 13, 'text-anchor': 'end', fill: 'var(--ink-3)' });
          t.textContent = row.label;
          tree.appendChild(t);

          // Uplinks to the tier above, thinned to show oversubscription.
          if (ri > 0) {
            const above = rows[ri - 1];
            const shown = Math.max(1, Math.round(6 / over));
            for (let i = 0; i < row.n; i++) {
              for (let j = 0; j < shown; j++) {
                const tgt = (i + j) % above.n;
                tree.appendChild(el('line', {
                  x1: 40 + i * w + w / 2, y1: row.y,
                  x2: 40 + tgt * (640 / above.n) + (640 / above.n) / 2, y2: above.y + 18,
                  stroke: 'var(--line-strong)', 'stroke-width': 0.7,
                  opacity: over === 1 ? 0.5 : 0.75,
                }));
              }
            }
          }
        });

        // Endpoints under the leaf row.
        const leafRow = rows[rows.length - 1];
        const w = 640 / leafRow.n;
        for (let i = 0; i < leafRow.n; i++) {
          for (let j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
            tree.appendChild(el('rect', { x: 40 + i * w + 4 + j * 10, y: leafRow.y + 30,
              width: 7, height: 14, rx: 2, fill: 'var(--gpu)', opacity: 0.7 }));
          }
          tree.appendChild(el('line', { x1: 40 + i * w + w / 2, y1: leafRow.y + 18,
            x2: 40 + i * w + w / 2, y2: leafRow.y + 30, stroke: 'var(--line-strong)',
            'stroke-width': 0.7, opacity: 0.5 }));
        }
        const epLabel = el('text', { x: 32, y: leafRow.y + 41, 'text-anchor': 'end', fill: 'var(--ink-3)' });
        epLabel.textContent = 'GPUs';
        tree.appendChild(epLabel);

        const note = el('text', { x: 360, y: leafRow.y + 66, 'text-anchor': 'middle', fill: 'var(--ink-3)' });
        note.textContent = 'schematic — widths are illustrative, the counts are in the readout';
        tree.appendChild(note);
      };

      const wireSeg = (attr: string, apply: (v: string) => void) => {
        fig.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>(`[data-${attr}]`).forEach((b) => {
          b.addEventListener('click', () => {
            apply(b.dataset[attr]!);
            fig.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>(`[data-${attr}]`).forEach((o) =>
              o.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(o === b)));
            render();
          });
        });
      };
      wireSeg('radix', (v) => { radix = Number(v); });
      wireSeg('tiers', (v) => { tiers = Number(v); });
      wireSeg('over', (v) => { over = Number(v); });

      render();
    }
  </script>
</Base>
